I compiled [38;5;10mc_src[0m with gcc 11.5 and probed glibc before writing anything, rather than guessing:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10m%a[0m: zero prints [38;5;10m0x0p+0[0m (not [38;5;10m0x0p-1022[0m); subnormals print leading digit [38;5;10m0[0m with the exponent pinned at [38;5;10mp-1022[0m; the 13 mantissa hex digits have trailing zeros stripped and the [38;5;10m.[0m vanishes when nothing remains. NaN/inf print [38;5;10mnan[0m/[38;5;10m-nan[0m/[38;5;10minf[0m/[38;5;10m-inf[0m with the sign taken from the sign bit.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10m%.4f[0m: delegated to Rust's exact fixed formatter. An exact decimal tie at 4 fractional digits is impossible for a binary double (it would need a factor of 5⁴ in the denominator), so glibc's round-half-even tie rule is never observable — verified on the full-precision expansions of [38;5;10m1e300[0m and [38;5;10mDBL_MAX[0m.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mscanf[0m quirks, all confirmed against the C binary and reproduced:[0m[0m
  - [38;5;10m"infinit"[0m → [1mfailure[22m (0.0), while [38;5;10m"inf"[0m, [38;5;10m"infy"[0m, [38;5;10m"infinityx"[0m → inf. Once glibc consumes a 4th character [38;5;10mi[0m it commits to [38;5;10m"infinity"[0m and cannot back off.[0m[0m
  - [38;5;10m"1e"[0m, [38;5;10m"1e+"[0m, [38;5;10m"0x1.8p+z"[0m succeed (incomplete exponent is dropped by [38;5;10mstrtod[0m).[0m[0m
  - [38;5;10m"-0x"[0m, [38;5;10m"-0xp1"[0m → failure leaving [38;5;10m+0.0[0m, but [38;5;10m"-0x."[0m → [38;5;10m-0.0[0m. This hinges on glibc requiring a mantissa hex digit or radix point after the [38;5;10m0x[0m; exponent digits don't satisfy it, and the exponent branch's internal [38;5;10mgot_dot = 1[0m must not be confused with a real radix point. Getting this wrong was the only mismatch the fuzzer found, in two rounds.[0m[0m
  - Hex-float parsing is done with my own u128 + sticky-bit accumulator and round-half-even, matching glibc on tie cases like [38;5;10m0x1.00000000000008p+0[0m → [38;5;10m1.0[0m vs [38;5;10m0x1.00000000000018p+0[0m → [38;5;10m0x1.0000000000002p+0[0m, and on subnormal ties.[0m[0m
